  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A much drier air mass will be in place through the \nend of the week and to start the weekend. \n\nDrier air continues to filter in from northwest to southeast early \nthis morning in the wake of a cold front. An impulse driving some \nshowers will exit southeastern New Jersey and southern Delaware \nearly this morning. The intensity of this activity has weakened \ngiven drier air undercutting it from the northwest.\n\nAn upper-level trough will then gradually weaken across the \nNortheast and northern Mid-Atlantic later today, tonight and Friday. \nSurface high pressure builds across the Northeast and down into the \nMid-Atlantic through the end of the week before shifting offshore of \nNew England on Saturday. A northerly flow will keep a much drier air \nmass across our area along with cooler temperatures. The lighter low-\nlevel flow due to high pressure in place will result in some \nenhancement to the sea/bay breezes later today and again on Friday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Shower and thunderstorm chances increase early next \nweek with the potential for areas of heavy rain and strong \nthunderstorms.\n\nAs a strong upper-level ridge expands into the Plains early next \nweek, northwesterly flow aloft will occur across much of the East. \nThis ridge looks to get pushed back some as a potentially strong \nupper-level trough in eastern Canada sends spokes of energy \nsoutheastward across the Great Lakes and Northeast. Some guidance \nhas stronger shortwave energy which results in the trough closing \noff across the Northeast with time during the first half of next \nweek. This will also drive surface low pressure and a cold front our \nway around the Tuesday time frame. Some convection from upstream may \ntry and get into parts of our area later Sunday, however this is \nless certain.\n\nIt looks like we may turn convectively active early next week, \nespecially Tuesday into Wednesday. While there are differences among \nthe model guidance on the timing and strength of the features diving \nsoutheastward early next week, the setup would tend to favor \nupstream convective development given northwesterly flow aloft. This \nwould be enhanced if the trough aloft closes off. Deeper moisture \nadvecting northward ahead of this feature with eventually colder air \naloft arriving may result in widespread showers and some \nthunderstorms. While the specifics are uncertain at this time range, \nsome areas of heavy rainfall and strong thunderstorms are possible \ngiven the overall synoptic setup that is forecast.",
